    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe endpoint now rejects owner emails on a disposable-domain blocklist with a 400, on both `POST /api/owners` and `PUT /api/owners/{ownerId}` (both route email through `OwnerEmail.normalize`).\n\n- **`OwnerEmail.java`** \u2014 after syntactic validation, extracts the domain from the normalized address and throws a new `DisposableEmailException` when it's one of `mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`.\n- **`DisposableEmailException.java`** (new, checked) \u2014 so it surfaces in the `throws` clause and can be routed by OfficeFloor escalation.\n- **`DisposableEmailExceptionHandler.java`** + **escalation YAML** (new) \u2014 responds `400` with `{\"errors\":[\"email\"]}`, mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailException` pattern.\n- **`ValidateOwnerFields` / `ValidateOwner`** \u2014 added `DisposableEmailException` to their `throws` clauses.\n\nI kept this as a distinct exception from `InvalidEmailException` (syntactic invalidity) since a disposable domain is a semantically different rejection, matching the codebase's one-exception-per-rule convention.",
